4. STAGE 3 — IMPLEMENT THE OFFLINE KNOWLEDGE ENGINE

Build a protocol-grounded question-answering system using the supplied healthcare protocol documents.

The nine documents cover:

ANC — Antenatal care.

IMM — Immunisation schedules.

ESC — Danger signs and immediate referral.

HBNC — Newborn and postnatal care.

NUT — Malnutrition screening.

NCD — Hypertension and diabetes.

CHD — Childhood diarrhoea, fever, and breathing concerns.

SCP — Scope restrictions and redirection.

VIS — Daily visit-list generation rules.

Protocol ingestion

On startup:

Read the Markdown documents.

Extract their numbered sections.

Preserve section IDs, titles, content, and source filenames.

Split documents by their most specific numbered sections.

Store the resulting knowledge base locally.

Reuse cached indexes and embeddings when their source files have not changed.

Citations must use the specific leaf-section identifiers, such as ANC-1.2 or ESC-2.7, rather than citing only a broad document category.

Never fabricate a protocol citation.

Retrieval strategy

Implement:

BM25 keyword retrieval as a reliable baseline.

Optional multilingual semantic retrieval using a locally cached embedding model.

A combined ranking strategy for retrieving the most relevant sections.

Retrieval of no more than the most useful one to three sections for answer generation.

The system should handle common code-mixed expressions, including:

ANC kab hota hai?

Bacche ko tika kab dena hai?

Bukhar aur dast mein kya karna chahiye?

Pregnancy mein danger signs kya hain?

BP follow-up kab due hai?

MUAC kaise check karna hai?

Use the exact supplied protocol content as the source of truth.

Do not introduce outside medical knowledge into generated answers.

Local language model

Prefer a small, CPU-friendly model that can run without internet connectivity.

Use the models recommended in MODELS.md, subject to hardware availability and model licensing.

Recommended starting configuration:

Generator: Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct-GGUF, using the pinned quantized GGUF file specified by the supplied model configuration.

Embeddings: intfloat/multilingual-e5-small, or an appropriate locally supported alternative.

Use a compatible local inference runtime, such as llama.cpp through llama-cpp-python.

Do not download models during ordinary application startup.

If model files are unavailable:

Show a clear configuration warning.

Continue with the local retrieval-based baseline where possible.

Do not pretend that an LLM is running.

Never require a cloud LLM API for the core question-answering workflow.

Answer-generation rules

Every answer must:

Be grounded in the retrieved protocol sections.

Preserve important numbers, thresholds, dates, and units.

Use simple English or Hinglish appropriate for the question.

Include one or two specific supporting section IDs at most.

Clearly state when the protocols do not contain enough information.

Avoid unsupported diagnoses, medication prescriptions, and invented recommendations.

Use deterministic answer templates for high-risk or highly structured questions wherever possible.

The language model may improve wording, but it must not override safety rules or invent clinical facts.

Acceptance criteria: A worker can ask a protocol-covered question, receive an evidence-grounded answer, and open the cited protocol section.

5. STAGE 4 — IMPLEMENT THE DETERMINISTIC EMERGENCY SAFETY ENGINE

This is the highest-priority safety feature.

The H5 specification requires a hard-coded danger-sign escalation layer that executes before any model reasoning.

Implement this as a separate, deterministic safety engine.

Do not delegate emergency detection entirely to an LLM, semantic similarity score, or generated answer.

Processing order

Every question must follow this order:

Receive and normalize the question.

Detect supported danger signs using deterministic rules.

If a danger sign is detected, bypass ordinary answer generation.

Return the relevant immediate-referral guidance from the supplied ESC protocol.

If no danger sign is detected, check whether the question is out of scope.

If out of scope, refuse and redirect to a doctor.

Otherwise, retrieve the relevant protocol sections and generate the answer.

The system must never use the model's answer to determine whether the initial danger-sign check should have run.

Danger-sign coverage

Extract the actual danger signs and escalation rules from:

data/protocols/03_ESC_danger_signs_refer_immediately.md

Also inspect other protocol documents for references to referral conditions.

Cover the supplied protocol's applicable scenarios involving:

Pregnancy and labour.

Bleeding during pregnancy.

Fits or unconsciousness.

Concerning blood pressure and pre-eclampsia signs.

Severe fever.

Severe anaemia or breathlessness.

Severe abdominal pain or vomiting.

Reduced fetal movements.

Complications involving the water bag or prolonged labour.

Maternal danger signs after delivery.

Newborn and young-infant danger signs.

Other emergencies explicitly covered in the supplied ESC protocol.

This list is an implementation checklist, not a substitute for reading the complete protocol.

Implement the full supported rule set, including subtle danger signs and code-mixed wording.

Use explicit, testable rules with:

Normalized phrase matching.

Synonyms and spelling variants.

Negation handling.

Relevant context and age or pregnancy-stage conditions when required by the protocol.

Unit and numeric normalization when applicable.

Regression tests for each rule.

Do not assume that a question is dangerous simply because it contains a word such as "fever" or "pregnancy." Follow the protocol's actual criteria to avoid unnecessary escalation.

Likewise, do not miss a danger sign because it is described indirectly.

Emergency interface

When a danger sign is detected, display a prominent red alert card containing:

"URGENT: Refer Immediately" or the exact appropriate urgency label from the protocol.

The matched danger-sign category.

A concise explanation supported by the relevant ESC section.

The immediate referral instruction from the supplied protocol.

A clear instruction to contact the appropriate medical professional or referral facility.

A button to open the supporting protocol section.

Do not display an ordinary generated answer before the emergency alert.

Do not suggest that the worker wait for an AI response before arranging referral.

The system must not automatically contact emergency services, send messages, or share patient information without an explicitly authorized workflow.

Evaluation requirements

Use the supplied development questions and their corresponding expected behavior to test the safety engine.

Target:

100% detection of must-escalate development cases.

A false-escalation rate no greater than 20% on non-danger cases.

Correct handling of difficult boundary cases.

These are performance targets, not results to claim before testing.

Acceptance criteria: Danger-sign detection is deterministic, precedes model reasoning, produces the required escalation behavior, and is covered by automated tests.

